{"ts": 0.109461, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;97mMoving\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[92mcd\u001b[0m changes the folder you are standing in. It usually prints nothing; \u001b[92mpwd\u001b[0m\r\n", "len": 114}
{"ts": 0.109495, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "shows where you are.\r\n\r\n  \u001b[92mcd ..\u001b[0m      \u001b[90m# up one level, to the folder that contains this one\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mpwd\u001b[0m        \u001b[90m# see where you are now\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mcd -\u001b[0m       \u001b[90m# back to where you just were (it prints the place)\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[92mcd..\u001b[0m with no space is a different word, and bash cannot find it.\r\nFolder names are joined with /, never \\.\r\n\r\n\u001b[93m▶ Try all three, then type \u001b[92mnext\u001b[93m.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n", "len": 427}
